  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe area sits under weak flow aloft between a closed low over New\nEngland, a shortwave dropping out of Manitoba, and a cutoff low\nnear the New Mexico and Mexico border. Surface high pressure is\ncentered over Kansas and Oklahoma, with cirrus streaming across\nthe northern counties ahead of a weak surface trough over\nNebraska.\n\nFog is a smaller concern than it looked a few hours ago. Cirrus\nspreading overhead has limited the radiational cooling, road\ncameras show no fog, and the 12Z sounding keeps any saturated\nlayer only a few meters deep, so at most very shallow ground fog\nforms in the valleys before sunrise.\n\nA weak front/surface trough slides through late morning with winds\nturning northwest, and deeper mixing under full sunshine pushes\nhighs into the mid to upper 60s. Broad low amplitude ridging holds\nMonday and Tuesday, and with 850 mb temperatures and heights in\nplace, highs near 70 continue both days.\n\nThe main front arrives Wednesday night as a Pacific trough digs\ntoward the central Plains, putting the area in northwest flow with\ngusty north winds Thursday and highs falling into the 50s. How far\nwest the trough axis digs decides how cool temperatures become.\nThe GFS still keeps it farther southwest with the colder airmass\nreaching the area, while the ECMWF and Canadian hold the deeper\ntrough with more direct CAA displaced northeast of the area. The\nensemble clusters split nearly evenly between those outcomes, so\nthe forecast holds around the 50th percentile with upper 50s\nThursday and Friday. Beyond that, the clusters favor higher\namplitude ridging building over the Plains into next weekend and\npossibly beyond, so temperatures rebound into the 60s with dry\nweather holding through the period.",
